## Why This Is Asked
The question probes practical prompt governance: how to prove provenance and detect tampering from model transformations in a multi-tenant setting. It tests attack awareness, deterministic auditing, and safe-guard design under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Watermarking robustness under paraphrase/translation
- Non-semantic embedding and tamper detection
- Audit logging for per-tenant prompts
- Edge-case handling and false positives

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal watermark prototype (JS)
function addWatermark(prompt, tag) {
  return prompt + " [WM:" + tag + "]";
}
function detectWatermark(text) {
  const m = text.match(/\[WM:([A-Za-z0-9._-]+)\]/);
  return m ? m[1] : null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate watermark robustness across languages/models?
- How would you scale to thousands of tenants with dynamic watermark policies?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design a lightweight, repeatable prompt-testing workflow and catch prompt drift early.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt versioning
- Drift metrics (embedding similarity, content guards)
- Reproducibility and logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype skeleton for drift check
function driftFlag(basePrompt, paraphrase) {
  const baseOut = model(basePrompt)
  const paraOut = model(paraphrase)
  const sim = cosineSimilarity(embed(baseOut), embed(paraOut))
  return sim < 0.8
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multi-language prompts?
- How would you integrate with CI to prevent drift before deploy?

